i-Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-224055/labs/game/main.ts:19:    return { ...defaults, ...saved, scores: saved.scores || {}, preferences: { ...defaults.preferences, ...saved.preferences } };\nWee-Fit-Retreat-Wii-Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-224055/labs/game/main.ts:36:sounds.enabled = save.preferences.sound;\nWee-Fit-Retreat-Wii-Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-224055/labs/game/main.ts:55:  return matchMedia('(hover: none) and (pointer: coarse)').matches && !Array.from(navigator.getGamepads?.() || []).some(Boolean);\nWee-Fit-Retreat-Wii-Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-224055/labs/game/main.ts:57:function render(): void {\nWee-Fit-Retreat-Wii-Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-224055/labs/game/main.ts:58:  document.documentElement.classList.toggle('reduced-motion', save.preferences.motion);\nWee-Fit-Retreat-Wii-Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-224055/labs/game/main.ts:59:  document.documentElement.classList.toggle('high-contrast', save.preferences.contrast);\nWee-Fit-Retreat-Wii-Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-224055/labs/game/main.ts:60:  const content = screen === 'welcome' ? welcomeView(isTouchOnly()) : screen === 'hub' ? hubView(dayIndex, activityIndex, save.unlocked, save.scores) : screen === 'playing' && session ? gameView(session, attract, lastInputType) : session ? resultView(session) : '';\nWee-Fit-Retreat-Wii-Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-224055/labs/game/main.ts:61:  root.innerHTML = content + (modal ? modalView(modal, save.preferences) : '');\nWee-Fit-Retreat-Wii-Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-224055/labs/game/main.ts:62:  if (screen === 'playing') updateGame();\nWee-Fit-Retreat-Wii-Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-224055/labs/game/main.ts:65:function openGame(preview = false): void {\nWee-Fit-Retreat-Wii-Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-224055/labs/game/main.ts:71:function finish(): void {\nWee-Fit-Retreat-Wii-Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-224055/labs/game/main.ts:79:function updateGame(): void {\nWee-Fit-Retreat-Wii-Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-224055/labs/game/main.ts:107:function doFoot(foot: 'left' | 'right' | 'both'): void {\nWee-Fit-Retreat-Wii-Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-224055/labs/game/main.ts:128:    if (window.confirm('Reset all retreat progress and scores?')) { save = structuredClone(defaults); sounds.enabled = save.preferences.sound; persist(); dayIndex = 0; activityIndex = 0; }\nWee-Fit-Retreat-Wii-Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-224055/labs/game/main.ts:131:  else if (command === 'start' || command === 'retry') { openGame(); return; }\nWee-Fit-Retreat-Wii-Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-224055/labs/game/main.ts:139:  save.preferences[key] = input.checked; sounds.enabled = save.preferences.sound; persist(); render();\nWee-Fit-Retreat-Wii-Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-224055/labs/game/main.ts:159:window.addEventListener('pointerdown', () => { if (attract) wake(); else lastInput = performance.now(); });\nWee-Fit-Retreat-Wii-Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-224055/labs/game/main.ts:185:  if (screen === 'welcome' && !modal && now - lastInput > 60000) { dayIndex = 0; activityIndex = 0; openGame(true); }\nWee-Fit-Retreat-Wii-Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-224055/labs/game/main.ts:194:    const settings = save.preferences.assist ? { ...DEFAULT_BALANCE, gain: DEFAULT_BALANCE.gain * .7, instability: DEFAULT_BALANCE.instability * .7, damping: DEFAULT_BALANCE.damping * 1.3 } : DEFAULT_BALANCE;\nWee-Fit-Retreat-Wii-Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-224055/labs/game/main.ts:196:    updateGame();\n--- renderer ---\n/**\n * @file The renderer (PLAN.md §6.7, WP 0.8 then WP 2.1): WebGPU or nothing, then everything WebGPU offers.\n * `createRenderer` asks for a WebGPU adapter before anything else and refuses to start without one (`GFX_NO_WEBGPU`,\n * naming the fix). It creates the device itself, with every adapter feature and the adapter's limits\n * (engine/gfx/caps.ts), counts the pipelines it builds (engine/gfx/pipelines.ts) and hands it to three.js's\n * `WebGPURenderer`, so three.js never requests an adapter of its own and its WebGL 2 fallback has nothing to fall back\n * from; `await renderer.init()` follows, then the backend assertion. It then reports the GPU (`info`: adapter,\n * features, limits), checks the feature registry (engine/gfx/features.ts, an advice code for every feature off), and\n * routes WebGPU's errors to the log: an uncaptured validation or memory error as `GFX_GPU_ERROR`, a lost device as\n * `GFX_DEVICE_LOST` (r182 has no `renderer.onError`; these are its two error routes).\n *\n * `frame(view)` draws one frame: it fits the drawing buffer to the canvas and `gfx.resolution`\n * (engine/gfx/resolution.ts) and the camera's aspect to it, warms up when the scene's shader key changed\n * (engine/gfx/warmup.ts; the frames until it is done are skipped, never drawn half-built), resets `renderer.info`,\n * starts a new node frame (`advanceFrame`, engine/gfx/pipelines.ts: r182 starts one only in its own animation-frame\n * loop, so a frame drawn off it would show a post pass's last picture), and draws with `view.draw` (a post pass) or\n * `render()`. `view.alpha` is the clock's interpolation alpha, kept for the\n * stats. A frame that builds a pipeline after the warm-up raises `GFX_COLD_PIPELINE`: it stalled. `stats()` gives\n * `renderer.info`'s counts with the pipeline counts, and `gpuMs`, the GPU's milliseconds a frame where the device has\n * timestamp queries (WP 11.2: the features resolve before the renderer is made, so it tracks timestamps when\n * `feature:timestamps` is on; engine/gfx/enhanced/timing.ts reads them), and tiled lighting is its lighting when\n * `feature:tiledLighting` is (WP 11.5). Draw with `render()`, never `renderAsync()`\n * (deprecated since r181; docs/THREE-DELTA.md). Game code meets none of this (doctrine: Quality under the hood).\n *\n * Invariants: a `Gfx` exists only on the WebGPU backend; every failure to get there is a `GfxError` with its code and\n * fix, and nothing is drawn, configured or allocated on the GPU before the adapter check passes. Rendering settings\n * are view settings: nothing here reaches the sim or the hash.\n *\n * @example\n * const refused = await requestWebGPU(undefined).catch((error: GfxError) => error.code); // 'GFX_NO_WEBGPU' in Node\n * @see tests/e2e/renderer.spec.ts\n * @see tests/e2e/hello.spec.ts\n * @see engine/gfx/renderer.test.ts\n */\n/// <reference types=\"@webgpu/types\" />\nimport { REVISION, WebGPURenderer, type PerspectiveCamera } from 'three/webgpu';\nimport { codeError, defineCodes, log as sharedLog, type Log } from '../core/log';\nimport type { Registry } from '../core/registry';\nimport { createSettings, type Settings } from '../core/settings';\nimport { readCaps, requestDevice, type GfxCaps } from './caps';\nimport { gpuTimingFor, lightingFor, resolveFeatures, type FeatureReport, type GpuParticles, type GpuTiming } from './features'; // prettier-ignore\nimport { advanceFrame, checkPinned, countPipelines, type PipelineCounter } from './pipelines';\nimport { createResolution, type Resolution, type ResolutionMode } from './resolution';\nimport { createWarmup, type Warmup, type WarmupView } from './warmup';\n\n/** The codes the renderer raises at startup, with their fixes (collected into docs/ERRORS.md by `x docs`). */\nexport const GFX_CODES = defineCodes('gfx', {\n  GFX_NO_WEBGPU: {\n    template: 'WebGPU is unavailable: {reason}',\n    fix: 'open the page in a browser with WebGPU and hardware acceleration on (Chrome or Edge 113+, Safari 26+); headless, launch Chromium with WEBGPU_FLAGS from tools/lib/browser.ts (PLAN.md §8.8)',\n    doc: 'Raised at startup, before anything touches the GPU, when the browser has no `navigator.gpu`, gives no adapter or device, or three.js starts another backend than WebGPU. WebGPU is the only renderer: there is no WebGL fallback (PLAN.md §6.7), so nothing starts, and a page shows this message with its fix.',\n  },\n});\n\n/** The codes a running renderer raises through the log. */\nexport const FRAME_CODES = defineCodes('gfx', {\n  GFX_GPU_ERROR: {\n    template: 'WebGPU refused a call ({kind}): {message}',\n    fix: 'fix the resource or pipeline the message names; GPU errors never change play, but what that call draws is missing or wrong',\n    doc: \"Recorded from the device's `uncapturederror` event (engine/gfx/renderer.ts): a validation, out-of-memory or internal error nothing caught.\",\n  },\n  GFX_DEVICE_LOST: {\n    template: 'the GPU device was lost ({reason}): {message}',\n    fix: 'reload the page; the sim runs on the CPU and is intact (capture it first with __engine.capture()), but nothing draws until the renderer starts again',\n  },\n  GFX_COLD_PIPELINE: {\n    template: '{count} GPU pipeline(s) were built while drawing frame {frame}, after the warm-up: the frame stalled',\n    fix: 'register what the frame drew for the first time with gfx.warmup.register(id, { objects }), or add what changed to the shader key with gfx.warmup.keyPart(name, part), so the warm-up builds it first',\n    doc: 'Advice from `frame()` (engine/gfx/renderer.ts): the pipeline counter (engine/gfx/pipelines.ts) saw a pipeline built during a drawn frame after the first warm-up, the stall PLAN.md §8.7 budgets at 0.',\n  },\n  GFX_WARMUP_FAILED: {\n    template: 'the warm-up failed: {message}',\n    fix: 'the cause is recorded with this error; frames draw without a warm-up meanwhile, building pipelines as they meet them',\n  },\n  GFX_BAD_ALPHA: {\n    template: 'the frame alpha must be a number from 0 to 1, not {alpha}',\n    fix: \"pass the clock's interpolation alpha (clock.advance(now).alpha), which is always from 0 to 1\",\n  },\n});\n\n/** A code the renderer raises at startup: a key of `GFX_CODES`. */\nexport type GfxCode = keyof typeof GFX_CODES;\n\n/** A renderer failure: its code, the message from the code's template, and the code's fix. */\nexport class GfxError extends Error {\n  override name = 'GfxError';\n  /** The code, as docs/ERRORS.md lists it. */\n  readonly code: GfxCode;\n  /** What to do about it, from the code's `fix`. */\n  readonly fix: string;\n\n  /** Fills the code's template with `values` (`{reason}` → `values.reason`). */\n  constructor(code: GfxCode, values: Record<string, string>) {\n    const { template, fix } = GFX_CODES[code];\n    super(template.replace(/\\{(\\w+)\\}/g, (marker, name: string) => values[name] ?? marker));\n    this.code = code;\n    this.fix = fix;\n  }\n}\n\n/** What the renderer reports about itself (the GPU half of `__engine.info()`, PLAN.md §8.3). */\nexport interface GfxInfo {\n  /** Always `WebGPU`: no other backend gets this far. */\n  backend: 'WebGPU';\n  /** The adapter's identity (on this platform: vendor `google`, architecture `swiftshader`, a fallback adapter). */\n  adapter: GfxCaps['adapter'];\n  /** The adapter's features, sorted; the device has every one of them. */\n  features: string[];\n  /** The device's limits, by name: the adapter's own, unless `GFX_DEFAULT_LIMITS` said otherwise. */\n  limits: Record<string, number>;\n  /** three.js's revision: `182`. */\n  three: string;\n  /** The ids of the features on (engine/gfx/features.ts). */\n  enabled: string[];\n  /** The advice codes of the features off: every downgrade taken. */\n  downgrades: string[];\n}\n\n/**\n * One frame to draw: the warm-up's view, plus the clock's interpolation alpha (1, the latest state, by default) and\n * the GPU's particles the post chain draws in a pass of their own (engine/gfx/enhanced/particles.ts, WP 11.3).\n */\nexport interface FrameView extends WarmupView {\n  alpha?: number;\n  particles?: GpuParticles;\n}\n\n/** What `frame()` did. */\nexport interface FrameReport {\n  /** Whether it drew; false while a warm-up runs. */\n  drawn: boolean;\n  /** Whether a warm-up is running. */\n  warming: boolean;\n  /** The alpha it drew at. */\n  alpha: number;\n  /** Pipelines built while drawing it: 0 after the warm-up, or it stalled. */\n  built: number;\n}\n\n/** The renderer's counts: the last frame's from `renderer.info`, the pipelines' since startup. */\nexport interface GfxStats {\n  /** Frames drawn. */\n  frames: number;\n  /** The last drawn frame's alpha. */\n  alpha: number;\n  /** The resolution mode (null before the first frame), and the drawing buffer's size. */\n  mode: ResolutionMode | null;\n  width: number;\n  height: number;\n  /** The picture's offset in drawn pixels, right and down: a snapped camera's remainder (resolution.ts `shift`). */\n  shift: readonly [number, number];\n  /** The last frame's draw calls (`renderer.info.render`). */\n  drawCalls: number;\n  /** The last frame's triangles. */\n  triangles: number;\n  /** The last frame's points. */\n  points: number;\n  /** The last frame's render passes. */\n  renders: number;\n  /** The last frame's compute dispatches. */\n  computeCalls: number;\n  /** Textures alive on the GPU (`renderer.info.memory`). */\n  textures: number;\n  /** Geometries alive on the GPU. */\n  geometries: number;\n  /** Pipelines built since startup (pipelines.ts). */\n  pipelines: number;\n  /** Shader modules compiled since startup. */\n  shaders: number;\n  /** Pipelines three.js holds cached now; null when unreadable. */\n  cachedPipelines: number | null;\n  /** Warm-ups run. */\n  warmUps: number;\n  /** Pipelines built outside warm-ups since the first began: 0 is the budget (§8.7); null before a warm-up. */\n  pipelinesAfterWarmUp: number | null;\n  /** The GPU's mean milliseconds a frame over the last 60 (timing.ts); null before the first, absent without timestamps. */\n  gpuMs?: number | null;\n}\n\n/** A started renderer: three.js's `WebGPURenderer`, its device, its report, and what draws a frame. */\n--- full task excerpts ---\n22-\n23-Working Environment and deliverable:\n24:Create a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-224055] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-224055].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\n25-\n26-Performance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\n--\n72-Create a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\n73-\n74:Include a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\n75-\n76-Attract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\n--\n91-\n92-## Source Information\n93:CRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n94-- Date: 2026-11-04 22:40:55\n95-- Source: the work it's based on\n--\n101-- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\n102-- Online: network features used, or none\n103:- Testing: testing parameters allowed\n104-- Research: research done, or none\n105-- Environment: Codex CLI (headless); Linux\n106-- Model: OpenAI, gpt-6.1-terra, reasoning effort max\n107:- Usage: write \"added on import\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\n108-\n109:Exit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\n110-{\n111:  \"sourceInformation\": {\"date\": \"\", \"source\": \"\", \"plan\": \"\", \"genre\": \"\", \"perspective\": \"\", \"tech\": \"\", \"artDirection\": \"\", \"animation\": \"\", \"audio\": \"\", \"online\": \"\", \"testing\": \"\", \"research\": \"\", \"environment\": \"\", \"model\": \"\", \"usage\": \"added on import\"},\n112:  \"testingFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\"],\n113:  \"engineFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\"],\n114:  \"promptFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\"],\n115-  \"aiHub\": {\n116-    \"name\": \"the game's title, as on its title screen\",\n--\n128-The catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\n129-\n130:Testing Feedback\n131-\n132:- How many issues did testing catch?\n133-- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\n134-- What was the most helpful test you ran?\n135-- What was the least helpful test you ran?\n136-\n137:Engine Feedback\n138-\n139-- What did you like most about the engine?\n--\n144-- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\n145-\n146:Prompt Feedback\n147-\n148-- What did you like most about this prompt?\n--- current progress ---\n# Wee Fit Retreat — progress\n\n## Current phase\n\n- The required my-3dge workspace is checked out at `2dbd829`.\n- The standalone `Wee-Fit-Retreat-Wii-Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-224055.html` builds successfully from `labs/game`.\n- Continue with engine integration, browser validation, and the required final handoff.\n\n## Implementation checklist\n\n- [x] Build activity progression, wobble simulation, controls, and local persistence.\n- [x] Add an original 2D island presentation, synthesized effects, and settings.\n- [ ] Investigate 3D engine and AI Hub ghost integration; the current build has neither.\n- [ ] Complete presentation, synthesized music, accessibility, and developer tools.\n- [ ] Run the headless and cross-viewport/browser checks; address discovered issues.\n- [ ] Complete the About/credits information and `exit-interview.json`.\n\n## Testing notes\n\n- Issues caught: not yet measured. Critical issues: not yet measured.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
