{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_729","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"printf '%s\\\\n' '--- task priorities ---'; rg -n -i 'AI Hub|harness|about|exit.interview|must|hand.?in|test|cut.line|noise|10 seconds' PLAN.md TASK.md | head -140; printf '%s\\\\n' '--- repo status ---'; git status --short; printf '%s\\\\n' '--- game files ---'; rg --files Wee-Fit-Retreat-Wii-Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-224055/labs/game | head -50\"","aggregated_output":"--- task priorities ---\nPLAN.md:7:**[tested]** marks what was actually run on this machine on 2026-11-04, against my-3dge at commit e7a1243. **[source]**\nPLAN.md:12:8 Screens · 9 Multiplayer · 10 Art · 11 Animation · 12 Audio · 13 Writing · 14 Tech · 15 Engine traps · 16 Testing ·\nPLAN.md:44:minigames; breathing; rhythm stepping; rep counting; a daily test that yields an \"age\"; a talking fitness peripheral\nPLAN.md:47:**Never take (this is an original game, not a recreation):** the word \"Wii\" anywhere in the game except the About\nPLAN.md:48:screen's Source line and the hub's source field; \"Fit Age\", \"Fit Credits\", \"Fit Piggy Bank\", \"Body Test\"; the source's\nPLAN.md:59:**Little Fulcrum**: a green and sand island about 200 m across, one low summit, a clean white retreat scattered on it,\nPLAN.md:120:accel   = gain × (target − cob) + instability × cob + noise(t) × (1 − calmEffect × calm) + disturbance\nPLAN.md:129:| `game.wobble.noise` | 3.5 /s² | 0–8 | Sway strength |\nPLAN.md:130:| `game.wobble.noiseRate` | 0.15 Hz | 0.05–0.4 | Sway speed: a slow push you lean against |\nPLAN.md:139:noise(t) = ( sin(2π·rate·m1·t + p1) + 0.6·sin(2π·rate·1.7·m2·t + p2) + 0.3·sin(2π·rate·3.1·m3·t + p3) ) / 1.3      // mi in 0.8–1.25\nPLAN.md:142:**These numbers are modelled, and the slow sway matters [tested in a small simulation of this exact model, 20 s, 10 seeds,\nPLAN.md:170:**Balance targets the tests enforce** (section 16) on 10 seeds at default settings, in every activity: the perfect bot\nPLAN.md:207:// actionMap:balance, every activity. [tested]: Space on both feet gives both held; values read move actions too.\nPLAN.md:249:**The Programme.** A five-day residency: a first full play takes about 45 minutes. Each day is a Morning Check plus three\nPLAN.md:282:**Saves** go in the page store (`createStore`, prefix unique to your game): day, done list, ages, pebbles, finds,\nPLAN.md:295:HUD. `bot` writes the same seat input a player does, and is used by tests, attract mode and dev auto-play. `short` is the\nPLAN.md:312:### Morning Check (about 75 s)\nPLAN.md:314:Tare's Platform. Tare asks one yes/no question for the day (it changes only the remark). Then three tests:\nPLAN.md:323:### Pose Class (about 2.5 min, three poses)\nPLAN.md:345:### Reps Hall (about 90 s, three exercises)\nPLAN.md:358:### Step Class (about 100 s)\nPLAN.md:394:### The Line (about 75 s)\nPLAN.md:406:about 1.2 Hz give +30% and a kick. Twelve buoy gates round the lagoon with seeded jitter. A ferry wake at gate 7 and a\nPLAN.md:415:gap in the rim. Score: share sunk × 800, plus time left. No Wobble noise here: this one is about touch.\nPLAN.md:420:**Air** (about 2 s): lean y spins you, lean x twists, scrubbing a rotation strip. Enter the water within 15° of vertical.\nPLAN.md:432:### Island Jog (about 150 s)\nPLAN.md:434:Follow Gerald round the coast path, a ring of flags past every landmark. Stick steers; alternating feet at about 2.5 Hz gives\nPLAN.md:435:+35% speed (swap `actor.character` for another `createCharacter` with the new speed **[tested]**). Stay within 8 m of him (\"the Pace Ribbon\").\nPLAN.md:445:## 7. The finale: The Great Tilt (about 4 minutes)\nPLAN.md:472:   closes them. Tare: \"Everything is balanced now. Please do not tell anyone about the rock.\" The last Morning Check. Credits, with\nPLAN.md:482:**10-foot rules (test them at 1920 × 1080):** body text at least 2.6vh (28 px), prompts and menus 3.2vh or more; one idea per\nPLAN.md:504:              └─ About / Credits\nPLAN.md:516:  Resume, Restart Activity, Back to the Island, Settings, Controls, Dev Mode, About / Credits, Quit to Title.\nPLAN.md:520:- **About / Credits:** paged, from one data file: Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, Prompt Feedback, credits\nPLAN.md:527:  from `inspector.describe('setting', path)` **[tested]**.\nPLAN.md:529:- **Cheats:** bot plays for me, no topple, unlock all, give pebbles, reset save, a throwaway save mode (`createStore` `sandbox`).\nPLAN.md:534:where you were. Set `game.auto` and restart the scene with `inspector.seed(n)` (restarts the sim, keeps settings **[tested]**).\nPLAN.md:536:Paddle Glide, Sand Garden, Step Class, Hoop Patrol, Cliff Plunge, then the Great Tilt's Centre Stone, about 15 s each, then round\nPLAN.md:541:Settings and never start play. A key or pad input at any time returns to normal. Always pass `touch=off` in the engine's query, or it draws its own touch stick **[source; the setting is accepted: tested]**.\nPLAN.md:552:Pond, each in its `short` 45 s form with a 6 s lead-in. About 2.7 minutes. Score: the sum, 0–3000. They all play on mats, so they need\nPLAN.md:564:### In AI Hub: AI Hub Session Protocol v1, mode **ghost**\nPLAN.md:567:`labs/game/hub/`: `session.js` (save it as `session-client.txt`), and for tests `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` from the same\nPLAN.md:587:**Startup** **[tested: this shape, on the starter's one scene, passed the harness at 2, 3 (host leaving) and 4 players with the engine running in every frame]**:\nPLAN.md:596:const watchdog = info ? setTimeout(() => AIHub.ready(), 14000) : 0;   // the harness allows 20 s after hello\nPLAN.md:601:**The biggest trap in this whole plan [tested]:** the hub opens the page with `?aihub=1&aihub-seed=N`. `createEngine` treats every\nPLAN.md:602:unknown query key as a setting and fails with `CORE_BAD_SETTING`, **and the harness still reports PASS**, because the client keeps\nPLAN.md:603:answering while the game is dead. Strip those keys, and make your test assert, inside every frame, `__engine.ready === true` and\nPLAN.md:611:  `keydown` on `window`: the harness sends a synthetic one. The card has no menus or choices, and draws numbers only from `AIHub.rng('card')`.\nPLAN.md:614:  `AIHub.progress({ score: 0, label: 'Breathing in' })` and one ghost message, so a slow copy still passes the harness.\nPLAN.md:627:- Outside a session (`connect` gives `null`, or no client at all in the dev server) the game must behave exactly as it does alone.\nPLAN.md:629:**Boot deadline, the one place performance matters.** The harness gives every copy 20 s to call `ready()`. **[tested on SwiftShader]** the starter\nPLAN.md:640:**The look [tested: this is the top-left picture of four tried, bright and clean]:**\nPLAN.md:661:**The island [tested]:** `defineTerrain` in the scene with `addTerrainBody`: 240 × 240 m, `cell: 2`, a dome radius about 110 and height about 11 plus a summit hill; `flats` for\nPLAN.md:664:**The sea [its look: tested]:** one huge flat prop at y = 2, a material using `map: { texture: 'texture:water', seed: 2, params: { base: '#6fd0e8' } }`, on a kinematic body. The texture is baked, so **drift the prop** slowly and bob it\nPLAN.md:672:Anything that moves goes on a kinematic body: `body: { type: 'kinematic', shape, size }` in the prop, then `driveBody(physicsOf(w), entity.sceneProp.body, position, rotation, w.dt)` each step, with full rotation **[tested]**. So the tray, hoops, board and sea pose freely. Physically simulated things are dynamic bodies. Anything else is drawn by HTML.\nPLAN.md:687:**The technique [tested: pictures showed a Heron with continuous two-axis lean, and a seated pose leaning the same way]:**\nPLAN.md:704:       // … LeanL, LeanF, LeanB the same way: the full-lean key should read about 12° on screen.\nPLAN.md:711:3. **Additive leans on top.** Play `LeanR`, `LeanL`, `LeanF`, `LeanB` with `additive: true` as well; their first key must be neutral (an additive clip is its change from its first frame).\nPLAN.md:721:   (`seekClip` only reaches the top clip, so it cannot do two axes.) `spawnActor`'s `clips: { sets: ['clipset:retreat'], play: … }` plays the first clip, and `playClip` works from the first step on. **In `setup` it throws `SIM_BAD_ARGUMENT` [tested].**\nPLAN.md:726:**Reuse from the engine's library** by copying the JSON of the clips you need into `labs/game/cast/clipdata/` with their source's `rest` (no dynamic imports sim-side; static JSON imports pass lint and Node **[tested]**). Find them with `node x anim find <words>`:\nPLAN.md:729:**Check by numbers:** `actionCheck` for actions, `animCheck` for poses in tests. For a look at a pose, use one screenshot, not many.\nPLAN.md:735:All sound is code and data. No audio files. Test: the built HTML holds no `data:audio`, `.mp3`, `.wav` or `.ogg`.\nPLAN.md:737:**Music, original, tracker notation** (`defineSong`; `node x new song`; test with `node x audio song:<id>`): light, slightly over-pleasant lobby music with plucks and soft marimba, never a melody from the source.\nPLAN.md:750:**Music is 10% by default:** right after `createAudio()`, call `audio.volume('music', 0.1)`. The engine's default is 0.5 **[source]**. Settings shows it as 10%. Test `audio.volume('music') === 0.1` on a fresh save. Mix each song so it is fine at 100% and a whisper at 10%; nothing the player needs may live only in music.\nPLAN.md:754:- A **syllable bank** per speaker: 16 entries (`defineSfx`), each a short consonant noise then a saw through two bandpass filters at a vowel's formants. **[tested: this renders, and vowels differ measurably; nobody can listen here, so judge by numbers]**\nPLAN.md:758:    { wave: 'noise', dur: 0.015, vol: 0.12, attack: 0.001, filter: { type: 'bandpass', freq: 3200 } },\nPLAN.md:778:**Effects** (`defineSfx`, plus built-ins `sfx:select`, `sfx:confirm`, `sfx:cancel`, `sfx:coin`, `sfx:step*`): wobble creak with pitch by `r`; breath in and out (filtered noise); mat squeak; stamp thump; pebble clack; small and huge splashes; gull squawk; frog ribbit; rope creak; marble roll, a loop with level by speed; hoop whoosh; clap, kick, hat; whistle; ferry horn; thunder; the island's deep groan, pitched by tilt; polite crowd \"ooh\". **Ambience:** sea, birds, wind, rain, as loops with `loop` passes.\nPLAN.md:788:**Voice.** Short declarative sentences in the second person. Polite, level, a beat too honest. Statements of fact that turn into mild judgement, then keep going as if nothing happened. \"Did you know?\" trivia that is not quite trivia. Yes/no questions nobody should be asking. Never sarcastic out loud, never cruel, never shouting, never an exclamation mark from Plumb. Never about bodies, only about wobble. Write at least 200 lines; none longer than two short sentences. Keep them in `labs/game/ui/lines/`, keyed by id; the sim emits ids, the page says them.\nPLAN.md:793:- \"Welcome to Little Fulcrum. I am Tare. I used to weigh people. We don't talk about that.\"\nPLAN.md:833:**Stack:** my-3dge full workspace (TypeScript, three.js r182 on WebGPU, Rapier 0.21, Vite, Vitest, Playwright, HTML/CSS UI). No new npm dependencies. The engine stays read-only: `npm run check` compares every engine file with `engine-lock.json`. Workarounds go in the game; what you needed goes in `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md`.\nPLAN.md:835:**Setup [tested], in the empty dev folder:**\nPLAN.md:841:npm test         # 5 s\nPLAN.md:848:Read `AGENTS.md` and `docs/GAME-GUIDE.md` once, as it asks. `docs/API.md` and the engine's file comments are for grepping. `labs/box/` is the worked example. Use `node x new <kind> labs/game/<dir>/<name>` for props, bodies, builds, actions, emitters, sfx, songs, looks and cameras: each comes with its test. Grow from the starter in `labs/game/`, and remove its gem game once your scenes replace it. `AGENTS.md` says to ask the owner about anything outward or costly: there is nobody to ask in this run, and nothing in this plan needs it, so take the safer choice and note it.\nPLAN.md:852:**The pilot.** Keep every character, the Guest included, at `player: false`, and let one sim system, `pilot`, write `actor.intents` each step: the step's `move` on the island, nothing in an activity, the bot's in attract, the slope added in the finale. This one rule makes roam, activities, attract and the Great Tilt the same code. Cameras follow only a `player: true` actor by themselves, so give them the Guest's feet with `createEngine({ cameraInput: () => ({ follow: feet }) })` **[tested: walks and the chase camera follows]**.\nPLAN.md:861:  1. Scene `actions` **[tested]**: `defineScene(id, { actions: { 'game.start': (w, args) => … } })`, called with `inspector.actions.run('game.start', { event: 'pond' })`. Runs between steps, recorded in replays. **Use this for every menu pick:** `game.begin` (with the save's state, since the sim cannot read the store), `game.start`, `game.leave`, `game.sleep`, `round.go`.\nPLAN.md:875:  ui/       screens/*.ts  hud/*.ts  glyphs.ts  lines/*.ts  dev/*.ts  theme.css  about-data.ts\nPLAN.md:884:**The single HTML [tested: 6.7 MB, 4 s, opens from file://, client stays byte-identical].** `node x html` is engine-only, so the game carries this. The session client goes in as text, so no formatter ever touches it: the hub rejects an edited copy by SHA-256.\nPLAN.md:916:Use a function as `replace`'s second argument, as here: the bundle holds `$` patterns that a string replacement would corrupt. Everything is inlined, so no CDN is needed. Run from the dev folder; write the deliverable where your TASK.md says. `npm run format` will restyle `tags.html` and this script, harmlessly **[tested: the build still passes and the client stays pristine]**; Prettier leaves the `.txt` alone.\nPLAN.md:918:**`window.__game`**, a small read-only state handle the page publishes for tests: screen, attract, last device, glyph set, volumes, day, event, focus.\nPLAN.md:926:| Hub's `?aihub=…` in the query | `CORE_BAD_SETTING`, engine dead, harness still passes | Strip them; assert `__engine.ready` in every frame |\nPLAN.md:931:| `node x new <kind>` reports failure in a game | `NEW_DOCS` lines about `labs/playground/…`: the engine's docs check tripping over its own guide. The entry is written and its test passes | Ignore those lines and don't touch the docs; note it in `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md` |\nPLAN.md:946:| `npm run dev` | Binds port 5173 strictly, on a machine other agents share; `PORT=0` falls back to 5173 **[source]** | Prefer the built file on `file://` and `node x shot`, which picks a free port. If you must, pick a free port for `PORT` |\nPLAN.md:948:| Fast sway | Doing nothing beats a human thumb **[tested]** | Keep `game.wobble.noiseRate` slow; judge with the delayed-thumb bot |\nPLAN.md:952:## 16. Testing\nPLAN.md:959:- **Model:** the Wobble Model's maths; no input stays in a 0.22 ring only part of the time (about 40%), a held full stick topples, `calm` lowers sway.\nPLAN.md:969:**Browser stages (the built file, on `file://` where possible).** Use the workspace's `@playwright/test` with `executablePath: process.env.CHROMIUM_PATH` and flags `--no-sandbox --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader`. Or Python Playwright, already installed. One or two browsers at once; close them. \"Target closed\" means memory: run fewer, don't retry blindly. Firefox and WebKit have no WebGPU here, so the game can only run in Chromium; use WebKit only to check the \"no WebGPU\" page.\nPLAN.md:979:- **About:** every Source Information field and every feedback answer is on the screen.\nPLAN.md:980:- **Standalone hub checks, as the hub's own test does:** press Enter, Space, Enter, Space; the client is there and inactive; `Math.random` is untouched; the screen is drawn; no errors.\nPLAN.md:981:- **Hub harness:** serve the built file beside `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` (Node `http` server or `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`, read the port it prints, confirm the page is your game, stop only that PID: never `pkill`, `killall` or `fuser -k`). Run `harness.html?game=<file>&players=N&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1&play=8000` for N = 2, 3, 4, then `players=3&…&scenario=host-leave`. Pass means `window.__hubCheck.pass`, with `body[data-complete=\"true\"]` at the end. **Also assert in each frame** `__engine.ready === true`, zero engine errors, a `ghost` entity at 2 players; no console line starting `[AIHub]`; and that the pasted client's SHA-256 from banner to last line matches `529eac78c54cfff0e60d375a9c63f66d1a594327ddcc262a2bdf9b1f2fb6582a` (client 1.4.0; compare what you fetched first, the hub may have moved on).\nPLAN.md:982:- **By hand in the harness:** run it once without `auto` at 2 players: its frames are your players on this computer, and clicking a frame gives it the keyboard. New round, Time up, Remove player. Drive it by script and read the state to see the card, ghosts and results; one screenshot.\nPLAN.md:984:**Screenshots, at the end, by eye, 1920 × 1080:** title, island, Pose Class HUD, Still Pond with a lure, The Line, Great Tilt wide shot, results, pause, Dev Mode, About. `node x shot labs/game --png` or a Playwright screenshot: both worked here.\nPLAN.md:992:1. **Skeleton (first hour's job).** Workspace, one-file build, hub client and tags, `game:round` with one mat, harness passing with the engine truly running, `verify.mjs` skeleton.\nPLAN.md:993:2. **The verb.** Wobble Model, action maps, mouse lean, Balance Disc, glyphs, one activity (Still Ring), its bot, tests, dev sliders. Tune until holding still is fun by itself. The bot tells you it is winnable; numbers tell you it isn't trivial.\nPLAN.md:1001:10. **The shell.** Attract, full Dev Mode, Settings, Controls, About, touch-only, no-WebGPU page.\nPLAN.md:1005:**Cut line, if and only if you run out of budget.** Cut whole things and re-flow `days.ts`, leaving no trace (no greyed-out rows, no mentions), in this order: Great Tilt step 3 → Expert variants and outfits → Hoop Patrol → Reps Hall → Island Jog's race (keep the walk) → Cliff Plunge → Step Class. Never cut: Morning Check, Pose Class, Still Pond, The Line, Sand Garden, Paddle Glide, Great Tilt steps 1, 2, 4 and 5, Group Class, hub ghost round, attract, Dev Mode, About.\nPLAN.md:1011:- Every feature in this plan is real: no TODOs, placeholders or stubs. `grep -rEi \"todo|fixme|placeholder|stub\" labs/game` is clean (except test words).\nPLAN.md:1013:- `node labs/game/tools/verify.mjs` reports zero failures; `npm run check` and `npm test` pass; the engine is unchanged or its patch is declared.\nPLAN.md:1021:Your TASK.md defines the chat reply, the About / Credits content and the exit interview, in its exact shape. Keep all the answers in one data file (`labs/game/ui/about-data.ts`), so the screen, the reply and the interview agree. They go in the bundle, so write them before the final build, then rerun `verify`.\nPLAN.md:1023:**Keep a running tally as you go** of the issues testing catches, and which were critical: you will be asked for both, and for your most and least helpful test. Note engine surprises in `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md` when they happen.\nPLAN.md:1025:Answers already decided: Plan: the name this file has in your folder. Genre and Perspective as in section 1. Tech: my-3dge at its pinned commit, three.js r182 on WebGPU, Rapier 0.21, Vite, TypeScript. Art: the engine's native toon puppets and generators with new retreat assets. Animation: the engine's clip layer and humanoid with a new readable clip set scrubbed from the sim. Audio: tracker songs, `defineSfx` effects, gibberish syllable voices, all code, music at 10%. Online: AI Hub Session Protocol v1, ghost; same-screen Group Class on its own. Environment and Model as your TASK.md gives them. Usage: \"added on import\".\nPLAN.md:1029:**Credits must include:** my-3dge (MIT), three.js (MIT), Rapier (Apache-2.0), @stdlib (Apache-2.0); the AI Hub session client. Animation clips by what you actually copied: Mesh2Motion and Quaternius Universal Animation Library (CC0; credit anyway); **100STYLE dataset, Mason, Starke and Komura, 2022, CC BY 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/), converted to key poses: required credit with a link and a note of changes** if you use any `STYLE100*` clip; CMU Graphics Lab Motion Capture Database if you use any `CMU` clip (terms in `data/anim/LICENSES/CMU.txt`).\nPLAN.md:1040:| Title: playful riff, picked by a real RNG from 50 | Yours, per TASK.md. This plan just says \"the game's title\": on the title screen, hub card, attract banner, About |\nPLAN.md:1050:| Testing: headless scripts, full playthrough, one command, failures only, sparing screenshots, no review subagents | 16 |\nPLAN.md:1059:| Source Information, feedback, About screen, chat reply, exit interview | 8, 19 |\nTASK.md:24:Create a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-224055] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-224055].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\nTASK.md:28:Testing: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\nTASK.md:50:No script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\nTASK.md:72:Create a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\nTASK.md:74:Include a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\nTASK.md:76:Attract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\nTASK.md:93:CRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\nTASK.md:103:- Testing: testing parameters allowed\nTASK.md:107:- Usage: write \"added on import\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\nTASK.md:109:Exit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\nTASK.md:111:  \"sourceInformation\": {\"date\": \"\", \"source\": \"\", \"plan\": \"\", \"genre\": \"\", \"perspective\": \"\", \"tech\": \"\", \"artDirection\": \"\", \"animation\": \"\", \"audio\": \"\", \"online\": \"\", \"testing\": \"\", \"research\": \"\", \"environment\": \"\", \"model\": \"\", \"usage\": \"added on import\"},\nTASK.md:112:  \"testingFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\"],\nTASK.md:130:Testing Feedback\nTASK.md:132:- How many issues did testing catch?\nTASK.md:134:- What was the most helpful test you ran?\nTASK.md:135:- What was the least helpful test you ran?\nTASK.md:139:- What did you like most about the engine?\nTASK.md:142:- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\nTASK.md:144:- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\nTASK.md:148:- What did you like most about this prompt?\n--- repo status ---\n M ../../../matrix.config.json\n M ../../claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261103-125824__x600c07__Wiretap-TheWire/result.json\n M ../../claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261103-183312__xf5fb84__SublimeCrisis3/result.json\n M ../../claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261103-183606__xf7f297__Bentou2/result.json\n M ../../claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261103-184315__x1295cd__KarateChampon/result.json\n M ../../claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261103-184947__xa320fb__DipoutHD/result.json\n M ../../claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261103-185013__xe162c9__OperationWolfpack/result.json\n M ../../claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261103-202616__x53986f__EyeOfTheBoltHolder/result.json\n M ../../claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261103-221326__x5b1962__DragonCrateBuilders/result.json\n M ../../clau